The opening display \(x+1=\{n+1\mid n\in x\}\) and \(x-1=\{n\mid n+1\in x\}\) is the tail of Exercise 2.20. Exercise 2.21 asks for a neighbourhood system \(\mathcal{C}\supseteq\mathcal{B}\) whose total elements include both the finite and the infinite sequences, distinguishing a total finite sequence such as \(01001\) from the partial element \(01001\bot\), and distinguishing the total empty sequence \(\Lambda\) from the undefined sequence \(\bot\), together with an approximable juxtaposition such that \(xy=x\) for every infinite total \(x\) and every \(y\), with a left-to-right bias when the left argument is not total. `memC` takes a neighbourhood to be a cone or a terminator singleton, `C` is the nested-or-disjoint system on that predicate with master the set of all finite strings, and `memB_imp_memC` includes every neighbourhood of \(\mathcal{B}\) because each of them is a cone. `singletonElt` is the total finite sequence \(\uparrow\{\sigma\}\), `isTotal_singletonElt` shows it is maximal because a neighbourhood of \(\mathcal{C}\) contained in a singleton equals that singleton (`subset_singleton_eq`), `Lambda` is \(\uparrow\{[]\}\), and `bot_lt_Lambda` shows that the bottom element, the principal filter of the master cone, lies strictly below \(\Lambda\). `juxtapose` relates \(X,Y,Z\) either by the cone clause, \(X=\mathrm{cone}\,\sigma\subseteq Z\), which ignores \(Y\), or by the terminator clause, \(X=\{\sigma\}\) and the prepend of \(\sigma\) to \(Y\) is contained in \(Z\). `juxtapose_singleton_mem`, which is `example_221`, says a neighbourhood lies in the juxtaposition of \(\uparrow\{\sigma\}\) with \(y\) exactly when it contains the prepend of \(\sigma\) to some neighbourhood of \(y\); a cone witness is rewritten through the master, since prepending \(\sigma\) to the set of all strings is the cone of \(\sigma\). `juxtapose_cone` says the juxtaposition of the partial element \(\uparrow(\mathrm{cone}\,\sigma)\) with any second argument is that same partial element, which is the bias for a non-total finite approximant. The infinite law \(xy=x\) is the observation that an infinite sequence is the directed supremum of those partial elements, and it is not a theorem; no lemma shows that such a sequence is total in \(\mathcal{C}\). **Theorem:** partial — \(\mathcal{C}\), the inclusion of \(\mathcal{B}\), the total finite sequences, the separation of \(\Lambda\) from \(\bot\), and the juxtaposition laws for a total finite left argument and for a partial cone are formalized, and the infinite left-zero law is not. **Proof:** partial — the nested-or-disjoint comparisons, the minimality of singletons, and the two membership arguments for `juxtapose` prove those laws; the directed-supremum reduction for an infinite left argument is not carried out.
